<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NOT PACKED, deliberately — same standing as OKF4net.Viewer, which the root
    README's project table annotates the same way.

    This project is the only one in the repository whose useful operation needs
    something that is not a NuGet dependency: a container engine binary
    (docker / podman / nerdctl) on the host's PATH, a daemon it can reach, and
    the ability to pull images. A package that restores cleanly and then fails
    at run time on every machine without that setup is a worse deliverable than
    no package. Consumers take it by ProjectReference, or vendor it.

    So there is deliberately no PackageId/Description/licence block here and no
    `Pack (Attestation.Containers)` step in release.yml. If that is ever
    revisited, both have to come back together — the state this replaces had a
    fully publish-configured .csproj and no pack step, which is neither shipped
    nor documented as unshipped.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OKF4net.Attestation\OKF4net.Attestation.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Lets the test project exercise internal members (JsonValues,
         CliContainerEngine's argument builder) directly. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="OKF4net.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
